  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 344 AM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\n===== Friday - Weekend =====\n\nTrend in forecast guidance has been slower on shifting the upper \nridge back to the west as an upper trough deepens over eastern \nCanada. While the upper ridge is expected to weaken some by the end \nof the week, the ridge axis is expected to remain over the Ohio \nValley on Friday. If this trend continues, it is not out of the \nquestion that that temperatures remain hot in the afternoon, current \nforecast is picking up on this slow transition with highs on Friday \nnow remaining in the upper 90s and heat index values ranging from \n102 to 107 or higher. While confidence is growing, it still remain \non the lower side. This is due to where we could see a weak sfc \nboundary set up over the region for Friday and where potential \nconvective complexes move through the Ohio Valley Friday afternoon \ninto Friday night. This placement of the boundary and potential \nconvective activity will play a role on how much clouds we could see \nas well as rain/storm chances. \n\nBy Saturday and heading into Sunday, the deepening upper trough and \nassociated upper low will slowly work southward into New England and \nthe Canadian Maritimes. This will allow the upper ridge to continue \nto retrograde further to the west and allow for slightly \"cooler\" \nair to advect in from the north-northeast. While we could be nearly \n10 degrees cooler this weekend than Thursday and Friday, that still \nmeans we would have highs in the 90s and upper 80s. We will also see \nan increase in shower and storm chances for Saturday into Sunday as \nthe aforementioned sfc boundary is expected to be somewhere over the \narea during this time. Due to the temporal and spatial difference in \ncurrent guidance, confidence remains low on the finer details for \nthis weekend.",
